 {"id": "intensive", "name": "Intensive", "q": "himself, the very", "prons": ["ipse"], "secs": "146, 298",
  "summary": "<i>Ipse, ipsa, ipsum</i> is the <b>intensive</b> pronoun: it adds emphasis to a person or thing, like English himself in the king <i>himself</i> came, or very in the <i>very</i> thing. The English word is the same as for the reflexive, but the function is different: <i>sē</i> is the object pointing back to the subject (he hurt himself); <i>ipse</i> just stresses who (he himself did it).",
  "pattern": "<b>Pattern:</b> declined like <i>ille</i>, but the neuter singular is <i>ipsum</i>, not <i>ipsud</i>. Genitive <i>ipsīus</i> and dative <i>ipsī</i> in every gender.",
  "basic": {"name": "Emphasis", "sec": "298.c",
   "rule": "<i>Ipse</i> adds emphasis to a noun or pronoun: the very, himself, herself, itself.",
   "how": "Read it as -self or the very, agreeing with the word it emphasizes.",
   "ex": [["id **ipsum**", "that very thing", "A&G §298.c"], ["tum **ipsum**", "at that very time", "A&G §298.c"], ["**Ipse** dīxit.", "He (the Master) said it.", "A&G §298.d"]]},
  "uses": [
   {"name": "Ipse and sē", "sec": "298.f", "lvl": "core",
    "what": "<i>Sē</i> means the object is the same as the subject (he hurt himself). <i>Ipse</i> emphasizes (he himself did it). Used with a reflexive, <i>ipse</i> agrees with the subject.",
    "clue": "<i>Mē ipse</i>: <i>ipse</i> is nominative, so it goes with I.",
    "ex": [["Mē **ipse** cōnsōlor.", "I console myself.", "A&G §298.f"]]},
   {"name": "Ipse without a noun", "sec": "298.d", "lvl": "core",
    "what": "On its own, <i>ipse</i> can mean he himself, pick out the main person (the master, the leader), or stress a subject left out of the sentence.",
    "clue": "A nominative <i>ipse</i> with no noun beside it.",
    "ex": [["vōbīscum **ipsī** recordāminī", "remember in your own minds", "A&G §298.d"]]},
   {"name": "Ipse for the reflexive", "sec": "300.b", "lvl": "adv",
    "what": "When a sentence reports what someone said or thought, <i>ipse</i> sometimes stands in for <i>sē</i> or <i>suus</i>, to make it clear who is meant.",
    "clue": "<i>Ipsīus, ipsī</i> in a clause where <i>suus</i> or <i>sibi</i> would be unclear.",
    "ex": [["cūr dē suā virtūte aut dē **ipsīus** dīligentiā dēspērārent", "why should they despair of their own courage or his diligence", "Caes. B.G. 1.40"]]},
  ]},
 {"id": "relative", "name": "Relative", "q": "who, which, that", "prons": ["qui"], "secs": "147, 303–308",
  "summary": "<i>Quī, quae, quod</i> is the <b>relative pronoun</b>: who, which, that. It starts a <b>relative clause</b>, a group of words with its own verb that describes a noun: the man <i>who is singing</i>, the city <i>which we saw</i>. The noun the clause describes is called the <b>antecedent</b>.",
  "pattern": "<b>Pattern:</b> genitive <i>cuius</i> and dative <i>cui</i> in every gender, and <i>quibus</i> for the dative and ablative plural. <i>Quae</i> is three things: feminine singular, feminine plural and neuter plural.",
  "basic": {"name": "Agreement with the antecedent", "sec": "305",
   "rule": "The <b>antecedent</b> is the noun the relative clause describes (from <i>antecēdere</i>, to go before, because it usually comes first): in the girl who sings, the antecedent is girl. The relative takes its <b>gender and number</b> from the antecedent, but its <b>case</b> from its own function inside the relative clause.",
   "how": "Find the antecedent to get the gender and number. Then ask what the relative does inside its own clause (subject, object, after a preposition) to explain the case.",
   "ex": [["Puella **quam** vidēs cantat.", "The girl whom you see is singing. (antecedent <i>puella</i>: feminine singular; accusative because it's the object of <i>vidēs</i>)", ""], ["Ea diēs **quam** cōnstituerat vēnit.", "That day which he had appointed came.", "A&G §305"], ["Aduatucī, dē **quibus** suprā dīximus", "the Aduatuci, of whom we have spoken above", "Caes. B.G. 2.29"]]},
  "uses": [
   {"name": "Antecedent left out", "sec": "307.c", "lvl": "core",
    "what": "The antecedent is often left out, especially when it would be someone or anyone: <i>quī</i>, (the man) who, he who.",
    "clue": "A relative clause with no noun for it to refer to.",
    "ex": [["**quī** decimae legiōnis aquilam ferēbat", "(the man) who carried the eagle of the tenth legion", "Caes. B.G. 4.25"]]},
   {"name": "Connecting relative", "sec": "308.f", "lvl": "core",
    "what": "A relative at the start of a sentence often links it to the sentence before, with the antecedent back in that sentence. Read it as and this, and he, this.",
    "clue": "A sentence that begins with a form of <i>quī</i> after a full stop or semicolon.",
    "ex": [["Caesar statuit exspectandam classem; **quae** ubi convēnit …", "Caesar decided he must wait for the fleet; and when this had come together …", "Caes. B.G. 3.14"]]},
   {"name": "Whoever: quīcumque, quisquis", "sec": "151", "lvl": "adv",
    "what": "<i>Quīcumque</i> (declined like <i>quī</i>) and <i>quisquis</i> (mostly <i>quisquis, quidquid, quōquō</i>) mean whoever, whatever.",
    "clue": "<i>-cumque</i> on a form of <i>quī</i>, or a doubled <i>quis</i>.", "ex": []},
  ]},
 {"id": "interrogative", "name": "Interrogative", "q": "who? what?", "prons": ["quis"], "secs": "148",
  "summary": "<i>Quis, quid</i> asks who? what? on its own. With a noun, the adjective <i>quī, quae, quod</i> asks which? what kind of?",
  "pattern": "<b>Pattern:</b> <i>quis</i> differs from the relative only in the nominative and accusative singular (<i>quis, quem, quid</i>). The rest, including the whole plural, is spelled like <i>quī, quae, quod</i>.",
  "basic": {"name": "Pronoun or adjective", "sec": "148",
   "rule": "<i>Quis</i> and <i>quid</i> stand alone: who? what? The adjective <i>quī, quae, quod</i> goes with a noun: which man? what kind of man?",
   "how": "If a noun in the same case stands next to it, it's the adjective.",
   "ex": [["**Quis** vocat?", "Who calls?", "A&G §148"], ["**Quī** homō vocat?", "What man calls?", "A&G §148"]]},
  "uses": [
   {"name": "Telling it from the relative", "sec": "148", "lvl": "core",
    "what": "<i>Cuius, cui, quem, quō</i> and the plural are shared with the relative. A question mark, or a verb of asking or knowing before the clause, shows it's a question (sometimes a reported one: he asked <i>whose</i> book it was).",
    "clue": "A question mark, or <i>rogō, quaerō, sciō</i> + a subjunctive verb (an <b>indirect question</b>: a question reported inside another sentence).",
    "ex": [["**Cuius** liber est?", "Whose book is it?", ""]]},
  ]},
 {"id": "indefinite", "name": "Indefinite", "q": "someone, a certain", "prons": ["aliquis", "quidam"], "secs": "149–151, 309–312",
  "summary": "An <b>indefinite</b> pronoun refers to someone or something without saying exactly who or what: someone, anyone, a certain man. Latin has <i>aliquis</i> (someone), <i>quīdam</i> (a certain one: the speaker knows who but doesn't say), and plain <i>quis</i> after <i>sī, nisi, num, nē</i> (anyone).",
  "pattern": "<b>Pattern:</b> these are <i>quis</i> or <i>quī</i> with something added. <i>Aliquis</i> uses <i>aliqua</i> instead of <i>aliquae</i> except in the nominative plural. <i>Quīdam</i> turns m into n before <i>-dam</i> (<i>quendam, quōrundam</i>), and has <i>quiddam</i> as a pronoun but <i>quoddam</i> as an adjective.",
  "basic": {"name": "Degrees of definiteness", "sec": "310",
   "rule": "<i>Quis</i> (any) is the least definite, <i>aliquis</i> (some) is in between, and <i>quīdam</i> (a certain) is the most definite: the speaker has someone particular in mind.",
   "how": "<i>Quīdam</i> refers to a particular person the speaker doesn't name; <i>aliquis</i> to someone unspecified.",
   "ex": [["dīxerit **quis**", "someone may say", "A&G §310"], ["**aliquī** philosophī ita putant", "some philosophers think so", "A&G §310"], ["habitant hīc **quaedam** mulierēs pauperculae", "some poor women live here", "Ter. Ad. 647"]]},
  "uses": [
   {"name": "After sī, nisi, num, nē", "sec": "310.a", "lvl": "core",
    "what": "Plain <i>quis</i> means any(one) and appears mainly after <i>sī, nisi, nē</i> and <i>num</i>: <i>sī quis</i>, if anyone; <i>nē quis</i>, so that no one.",
    "clue": "<i>Sī, nisi, num</i> or <i>nē</i> followed by <i>quis</i> (not <i>aliquis</i>).",
    "ex": [["sī **quis**", "if anyone", ""], ["nē **quis**", "so that no one", ""]]},
   {"name": "In negative sentences: quisquam, ūllus", "sec": "311", "lvl": "core",
    "what": "In negative sentences, any(one) at all is <i>quisquam</i> (pronoun) or <i>ūllus</i> (adjective).",
    "clue": "<i>Numquam, nōn, sine</i> + <i>quisquam</i> or <i>ūllus</i>.",
    "ex": [["Iūstitia numquam nocet **cuiquam**.", "Justice never harms anybody.", "Cic. Fin. 1.50"], ["sine **ūllō** metū", "without any fear", "A&G §311"]]},
   {"name": "Each: quisque", "sec": "151", "lvl": "adv",
    "what": "<i>Quisque, quidque</i> (adjective <i>quīque, quaeque, quodque</i>) means each, every; <i>ūnusquisque</i>, every single one, declines both parts.",
    "clue": "<i>-que</i> on a form of <i>quis</i> that isn't and.", "ex": []},
  ]},
